children.translated_text:
) Then <s>Śakra</s>, lord of the gods, having understood that supremely wondrous and exceedingly difficult resolve of <s>Mādrī</s> and the <s>Bodhisattva</s>, surrounded by hosts of the thirty gods, approached that hermitage through the sky above, illuminated that forest with a magnificent radiance, and standing in the vault of heaven, spoke to the <s>Bodhisattva</s>:
In this deluded world, whose minds are bewildered by foolish and wicked thoughts,
O lord, attached to pleasures, bound by the snares of love for children and dear ones—

enduring all,
you alone, free from attachment, renounce your daughter who inspires affection;
surely you shall attain the state that is secure, peaceful, pure, and stainless

body.translated_text:
Then the <s>Bodhisattva</s> spoke to <s>Mādrī</s>:
Do not grieve for me here on earth, O <s>Mādrī</s>, for I seek the imperishable state free from sorrow;
follow this <s>brāhmaṇa</s> without sorrow, while I, like a deer

children.translated_text:
having resolved, glad, pleased, and delighted in mind, he reflected:
This gift in this forest shall be my last;
having renounced beloved <s>Mādrī</s>, I shall become free of all possessions

by the left hand, he spoke to the <s>Bodhisattva</s>:
I give this <s>Mādrī</s> back to you as your attendant;
she must never be given to anyone else, for betrayal of a trust
